    <body>
      <sec id="article-22821.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair encompasses laparoscopic and robotic techniques, most commonly the transabdominal preperitoneal (TAPP) and totally extraperitoneal (TEP) approaches. These procedures access the preperitoneal space to place a prosthetic mesh that covers the entire myopectineal orifice, thereby addressing direct, indirect, and femoral hernia defects. Compared with traditional open repair, minimally invasive techniques offer advantages, including reduced postoperative pain, faster recovery, earlier return to normal activity, and the ability to evaluate and repair bilateral hernias through a single access. Successful outcomes depend on precise knowledge of preperitoneal anatomy, careful dissection to protect neurovascular structures, and appropriate mesh selection and fixation.</p>
        <p>Clinicians participating in this course gain a comprehensive understanding of the indications, patient selection, operative anatomy, and technical steps involved in minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair. The activity reviews TEP and TAPP techniques, including port placement, preperitoneal dissection, mesh deployment, and complication avoidance. Participants also learn strategies for managing intraoperative challenges, recognizing complications, and incorporating robotic technology into practice. Emphasis is placed on evidence-based decision-making and optimizing patient outcomes through appropriate technique selection and multidisciplinary perioperative care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Screen patients for risk factors, contraindications, and appropriate surgical candidacy for&#x000a0;minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;key anatomical landmarks within the myopectineal orifice critical to successful hernia repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess&#x000a0;potential complications and intraoperative challenges unique to&#x000a0;minimally invasive&#x000a0;inguinal hernia repair.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate&#x000a0;surgical plans and postoperative expectations effectively with patients and interprofessional care teams to improve patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=22821&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=22821">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Approximately 20 million inguinal hernia repairs are performed worldwide each year, with 800,000 commencing in the United States. About&#x000a0;27% of men and 3% of women undergo inguinal hernia repair in their lifetime. More than half of these are repaired using minimally invasive techniques, and about one-fifth of those are now performed robotically.</p>
        <p>Within the myopectineal orifice,&#x000a0;inguinal hernias make up 96% of hernias&#x000a0;undergoing operative repair, and femoral hernias comprise&#x000a0;the remaining 4% of hernias.&#x000a0;Femoral hernias occur more frequently in women and have a higher likelihood of incarceration at presentation. Inguinal hernias may be acquired or congenital, and the risk of a symptomatic hernia increases with intraabdominal pressure or body mass index, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, smoking, connective tissue disease, peritoneal dialysis, an aortic aneurysm, or previous surgery (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Variants of Indirect Inguinal Hernia). The diagnosis of an inguinal hernia is often made through physical examination, but can be verified with ultrasound, computed tomography, or other imaging modalities (see&#x000a0;<bold>Images. </bold>Bilateral Direct Inguinal Hernias;&#x000a0;Computed Tomography of an Inguinal Hernia; and Computed Tomography of a Ureteroinguinal&#x000a0;Hernia).&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Treatment options for inguinal hernias include watchful waiting, nonoperative interventions, or surgical management. Traditionally, inguinal hernias were repaired through an open approach using native tissue to close the defect. This practice was followed by the use of prosthetic mesh to achieve a tension-free repair, which later evolved into laparoscopy with a variety of mesh and fixation options.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;2 approaches commonly&#x000a0;used in laparoscopic inguinal hernia surgery are the transabdominal preperitoneal (TAPP) and the totally extraperitoneal (TEP) repair. TAPP&#x000a0;accesses the peritoneal space within the abdomen, then creates a preperitoneal flap to access the preperitoneal space for hernia reduction and mesh placement. TEP&#x000a0;is performed&#x000a0;solely within the preperitoneal space.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The TEP approach limits intraabdominal injury, but TAPP allows&#x000a0;ready assessment of&#x000a0;the intraabdominal region. TEP is more technically demanding and&#x000a0;is associated with a steeper learning curve, but both techniques are comparable&#x000a0;with respect to recurrence, chronic pain, and adverse events. Several modifications, mesh fixation options, trocar placements, and strategies for managing rare and bilateral hernias are available in both approaches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair&#x000a0;is associated with quicker recovery, less postoperative and chronic pain, and fewer wound infections compared with open surgery. Laparoscopy allows&#x000a0;a clear view of the myopectineal orifice and surrounding structures, and is used successfully even in cases of incarceration or strangulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;There is a longer learning period to master laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair and to become familiar with inguinal anatomy from a laparoscopic perspective. Approximately 32 cases are&#x000a0;required to achieve proficiency. Open&#x000a0;repair can be performed with general, regional, or local anesthesia, whereas laparoscopic repair is conducted under general anesthesia. The choice of surgical approach is based on&#x000a0;factors including comorbidities, body mass index, recurrence, and bilaterality, as well as surgeon preference and proficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Robotic surgery offers&#x000a0;high-definition 3-dimensional visualization and potentially improved ergonomics compared with laparoscopic or open approaches. The instrumentation&#x000a0;polyarticulates and, together with tremor control, enables precise manipulation. Both&#x000a0;TAPP and&#x000a0;TEP approaches have been developed within robotic systems; TAPP is most commonly used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Since the implementation of robotic-assisted surgery, its utility has expanded in a range of surgical cases, including abdominal wall hernia repair. However,&#x000a0;robotics can be more costly and time-consuming, and only offers a small reduction in pain and return-to-work intervals. Hernia recurrence and complication rates are not significantly different from those after laparoscopic repair, although the learning curve may be shorter with the robotic approach.</p>
        <p>Although the robotic approach has not shown major clinical benefits to date, its adoption has been widespread and is thought to have driven the large increase in the use of minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r7">[7]</xref> Study results have demonstrated&#x000a0;an advantage of the robotic approach in complex and recurrent hernias. The use of robotics in surgery will likely continue to expand as technological sophistication advances.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>The minimally invasive surgeon must understand&#x000a0;inguinal&#x000a0;anatomy from multiple vantage points. This discussion will focus on the anatomy as viewed from the preperitoneal and intraabdominal perspectives. The inguinal area is defined by anatomic regions that contain critical structures. The myopectineal orifice&#x000a0;encompasses the anatomic area in which inguinal and femoral hernias are found.</p>
        <p>Medially, the myopectineal orifice is defined&#x000a0;by the lateral border of the rectus abdominus&#x000a0;muscle,&#x000a0;laterally by the iliopsoas muscle. The transverse and internal oblique arching fibers form the superior aspect, and the ileopectineal line and pectineal (Cooper) ligament form the inferior margin. The space is divided into superior and inferior aspects by the inguinal ligament. The femoral canal is inferior to the inguinal ligament and lateral to the pubic tubercle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>The inguinal ligament, the floor of the inguinal canal, extends from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle.&#x000a0;The ligament is composed of fibers from the aponeuroses of the lateral abdominal wall muscles, including the inferior edge of the external oblique aponeurosis. The lateral extension forms the iliopubic tract, made from thickened transversalis fascia.&#x000a0;The floor of the inguinal canal is&#x000a0;also reinforced medially by the thickened medial lacunar ligament.</p>
        <p>The pectineal (Cooper) ligament delineates the inferior margin of this area, extending along the pectineal line at the superior pubic ramus, and serving as an attachment for the iliopubic tract. Fibers form the anterior wall of the inguinal canal from the internal and external oblique aponeuroses.&#x000a0;The posterior wall is formed from the transversalis fascia and&#x000a0;a fusion of the transversus abdominus and internal oblique (conjoint tendon). The conjoint tendon fibers arch to form the roof of the inguinal canal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>The deep inguinal ring is&#x000a0;the opening in the transversalis fascia that marks the beginning of the inguinal canal, and is the space through which indirect hernia contents pass. The round ligament or spermatic cord and the ilioinguinal nerve enter the deep ring and exit the superficial inguinal ring.&#x000a0;The superficial ring, superior to the pubic tubercle, marks the end of the inguinal canal and is formed by fibers of the external oblique muscle and aponeurosis, which together form a triangular space that resists widening.</p>
        <p>The spermatic cord contains the vas deferens, the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, and the ilioinguinal nerve. Direct hernias pass through an attenuated abdominal wall medial and inferior to the deep ring. The inferior epigastric vessels, originating from the external iliac artery, are found medial to the deep inguinal ring and serve as a useful landmark to help discern direct and indirect inguinal hernias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Regions within the dissection areas of a&#x000a0;minimally invasive hernia repair that contain critical structures are known colloquially as triangles of pain and doom.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;The triangle of doom contains the iliac artery and vein, the deep circumflex vein, and, deeper, the femoral nerve. Borders include&#x000a0;the vas deferens medially, the gonadal vessels laterally, and the peritoneal fold inferiorly.</p>
        <p>The triangle of pain, which contains the femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve and lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, is bound by the iliopubic tract superiorly, gonadal vessels medially, and the peritoneal fold inferiorly. Care is needed during dissection and mesh fixation to prevent major vascular or nerve injury that could result in hemorrhage or chronic&#x000a0;pain. A femoral hernia&#x000a0;may be found incidentally during inguinal hernia repair.</p>
        <p>The location will be inferior to the inguinal ligament and medial to the femoral vein, which may be compressed.&#x000a0;Preparing a wide area for mesh placement is an important operative step. The spaces between the bladder and pubis (Retzius) and lateral (Borgros), superficial to transversalis fascia, extending from the inferior epigastric vessels to the iliac spine, are cleared of loose connective tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Minimally invasive&#x000a0;inguinal hernia repair&#x000a0;can be used for the repair of an inguinal or femoral hernia for any person for whom general anesthesia can be employed, and&#x000a0;insufflation can be safely performed. At its inception, the laparoscopic approach was more commonly reserved for bilateral and recurrent hernias, but it is now offered more routinely. Laparoscopy offers smaller incisions and the ability to visualize a greater area within the preperitoneal or intraabdominal compartments. For a recurrent hernia initially repaired through an open incision, a laparoscopic approach allows for intervention within tissue planes free from scar tissue.</p>
        <p>Additional benefits of the laparoscopic approach include a quicker postoperative recovery and a lower incidence of long-term pain. Likewise, robotic inguinal hernia repair confers the benefits of laparoscopy, with enhanced visualization and fine motor movements. While robotics remains more costly due to equipment and, in many circumstances, longer operative times, with greater experience, the duration of surgery has been declining, and definitive benefits have been demonstrated in complex reoperations and bilateral hernias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair may not be performed in those&#x000a0;who cannot&#x000a0;undergo general anesthesia or tolerate insufflation. Intraabdominal and pelvic adhesions, radiation, and ascites increase the risk of&#x000a0;a minimally invasive&#x000a0;approach. Benign prostatic hypertrophy, if pronounced, may impede&#x000a0;minimally invasive repair.</p>
        <p>Finally,&#x000a0;it may be more difficult to repair&#x000a0;large inguinoscrotal hernias or hernias that are incarcerated or strangulated, particularly if the surgeon is relatively&#x000a0;inexperienced in&#x000a0;minimally invasive repair.&#x000a0;Persons on anticoagulation&#x000a0;may be at increased risk of postoperative retroperitoneal bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients with recurrent inguinal hernias following&#x000a0;minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair, open repairs are generally recommended;&#x000a0;some surgeons do not use robotic approaches, despite the improved visualization and dexterity robotic technology affords.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Specific instrumentation&#x000a0;is left to the surgeon's discretion. General&#x000a0;laparoscopic equipment&#x000a0;includes:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Laparoscopic setup, including 30-degree camera, light source, and 2 monitors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Laparoscopic atraumatic graspers, scissors, hook,&#x000a0;and cautery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Laparoscopic suction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mesh of choice, self-gripping or non-self-gripping (an uncoated polypropylene or polyester mesh that is at least 10 x 12 cm)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fixation method of choice (tackers [absorbable/nonabsorbable], glue, suture)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Closing supplies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Open tray available as needed</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The number of robotic systems and applications in the surgical field is growing, with the technology evolving rapidly. Examples of robotic systems include the Saroa Surgical System (RIVERFIELD Inc), the da Vinci Surgical System family (Intuitive Surgical, Inc), including the da Vinci Si, da Vinci X, and da Vinci Xi platforms; the Versius Surgical Robotic System (CMR Surgical Ltd); the Hugo robotic-assisted surgery (RAS) system (Medtronic plc); the Dexter Surgical Robot (Distalmotion SA); the Avatera Surgical System (Avateramedical GmbH); and the MUSA Microsurgical Robot (MicroSure BV).&#x000a0;Laparoscopic equipment may be used during robotic-assisted surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Robotic equipment needed includes:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Laparoscopic/robotic ports</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Robot console and arms</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Operating station</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Robotic graspers, scissors, with bipolar and monopolar cautery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Robotic needle drivers</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mesh/fixation (suture/glue/tacks)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Closing supplies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Open tray available as needed</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Personnel include the surgeon and assistant, the anesthesiology team, and the operating room team. One potential advantage of a robotic approach to inguinal hernia repair is that it allows the surgeon to control 2 to 3 robotic arms and the camera, potentially eliminating the need for a surgical assistant.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Preparation for a minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair is similar to that for any other surgical/laparoscopic procedure. Preoperative antibiotics&#x000a0;are given per&#x000a0;institutional protocol.&#x000a0;General anesthesia is induced, and the patient is&#x000a0;placed supine with both arms tucked.&#x000a0;A Foley catheter is placed as needed to drain a full bladder.&#x000a0;Robotic-assisted inguinal hernia repair requires programming of the robot per the manufacturer's specifications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>The TAPP and TEP approaches differ primarily in how the surgeon accesses the preperitoneal space. In the TAPP technique, access to the preperitoneal plane is achieved through the peritoneal cavity, whereas the TEP technique enters the preperitoneal space directly without breaching the peritoneum. During TAPP repair, the peritoneum is incised around the medial umbilical ligament to create a peritoneal flap that allows entry into the preperitoneal space; this flap is closed at the conclusion of the repair. In contrast, the TEP approach establishes the working space through direct insufflation of the preperitoneal compartment. Port placement also differs between techniques.</p>
        <p>In TEP repair,&#x000a0;3 trocars are typically placed in a linear configuration along the midline from the pubis toward the umbilicus. In contrast, during TAPP repair, 3 ports are positioned transversely: a camera port at the umbilicus and&#x000a0;2 working ports along the bilateral midclavicular lines. Both approaches allow evaluation and repair of bilateral inguinal hernias. In each method, the myopectineal orifice is covered with a large prosthetic mesh that protects the direct, indirect, and femoral spaces.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r22">[22]</xref> When performed by experienced surgeons, both techniques are effective and yield comparable clinical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The operation begins with induction of general anesthesia, safe tucking of the arms, and wide surgical preparation. The patient is draped with a narrow midline exposure for TEP repair and a broader abdominal exposure for TAPP repair. A 10-mm umbilical trocar is inserted to facilitate mesh placement. In TAPP procedures, this trocar is positioned intraabdominally.</p>
        <p>In contrast, in TEP repair, the surgeon first creates access to the preperitoneal space through a small transverse incision in the anterior rectus sheath. The rectus muscle is retracted anterolaterally to expose the posterior sheath, and a balloon dissector may be introduced to develop an initial working space, typically displacing the peritoneum inferiorly. Alternatively, a trocar may be inserted under direct visualization, followed by gas insufflation and blunt dissection using a 0-degree laparoscope to develop the preperitoneal plane. Telescopic dissection may also be performed by gently advancing an instrument toward the pubis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>After the space is created, the initial instrument is replaced with a 10-mm trocar, and insufflation is initiated, with pressure maintained between 12 and 15 mm Hg. Additional midline trocars are then placed under direct visualization approximately&#x000a0;2 fingerbreadths above the pubis and midway between the inferior and periumbilical trocars. Accidental peritoneal entry during TEP repair can allow gas to escape into the abdominal cavity, resulting in loss of working space and necessitating closure with laparoscopic staples or sutures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>For both approaches, careful identification of key anatomic landmarks is essential. The lateral umbilical ligament, containing the inferior epigastric vessels, and the iliopubic tract extending from the pubis to the anterior superior iliac spine are identified to guide recognition of direct and indirect hernias. The region inferior to the iliopubic tract, medial to the femoral vein, and superior to the pectineal ligament is inspected for a femoral hernia.</p>
        <p>The preperitoneal space is cleared of loose connective tissue medially beyond the pubis, laterally to the anterior superior iliac spine, superiorly approximately 2 cm above the conjoint tendon, and inferiorly roughly 2 cm below the pectineal ligament. Dissection extends laterally toward the spermatic cord and into the space between the bladder and pubis. Lateral dissection is maintained superficial to the transversalis fascia to protect the iliohypogastric nerve, and care is taken to preserve the genitofemoral nerve along the psoas muscle.</p>
        <p>The hernia sac is separated from the internal ring or abdominal wall with blunt and sharp dissection, using electrocautery sparingly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r27">[27]</xref> Attention is required around the pectineal ligament to avoid injury to bridging vessels between the epigastric and obturator circulations. The contralateral myopectineal orifice may also be explored to evaluate for bilateral hernias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>In the TAPP approach, following intraabdominal insufflation, two 5-mm trocars are placed at the lateral borders of the rectus muscles in a horizontal line with the umbilical trocar. Adhesiolysis is performed as necessary. A peritoneal flap is then created approximately 4 to 5 cm above the internal inguinal ring, extending from the medial umbilical ligament laterally toward the anterior superior iliac spine and then inferiorly along the medial umbilical ligament. The flap is developed inferiorly to the pubis and iliac vessels while freeing the peritoneum from attachments to the cord structures, hernia sac, and any cord lipoma.</p>
        <p>Dissection proceeds beyond the point where the vas deferens and testicular vessels diverge, creating separate flaps in the setting of bilateral hernias. A pocket is developed between the peritoneum and the transversalis fascia, revealing the psoas muscle and the myopectineal orifice. The dissection is extended medially to the Cooper ligament and approximately 2 cm below the pubic symphysis. Direct hernia sacs are dissected from cord structures with gentle traction and electrocautery, and in female patients, the round ligament may be divided away from the internal ring when necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Once dissection is complete and the hernia sac is reduced with adequate hemostasis achieved, a prosthetic mesh, typically measuring approximately 12 &#x000d7; 15 cm, is introduced through the 10-mm trocar and positioned to cover the entire myopectineal orifice. Self-adherent mesh or standard polypropylene mesh may be used. If fixation is required, tacks are placed into stable structures such as the pubis, the aponeurotic arch, the pectineal ligament, and the lateral abdominal wall.</p>
        <p>Fixation may be performed using permanent or absorbable tacks or staples, and tissue adhesives such as cyanoacrylate glue or fibrin sealant may also be used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;In TAPP procedures, the operation concludes with closure of the peritoneal flap using a running suture. The abdomen or preperitoneal space is then desufflated while applying gentle manual pressure to reduce inguinal swelling, and all port sites are closed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Robotic-assisted inguinal hernia repair follows similar operative principles but incorporates robotic instrumentation. An 8-mm trocar is first placed in the left upper quadrant to insufflate the abdomen, followed by placement of an 8-mm midline trocar inferior to the umbilicus (approximately 15 cm above the pubic symphysis) and a third trocar in the right upper quadrant. The patient is positioned in a 10- to 20-degree Trendelenburg position, and the robotic system is docked.</p>
        <p>A 30-degree robotic camera is inserted through the midline port, while graspers and scissors are introduced through the lateral trocars. The robotic platform provides&#x000a0;3D visualization and wristed instrumentation that facilitates precise tissue manipulation and suturing. After docking, the surgeon operates from the console, performing dissection and mesh placement as in the laparoscopic TAPP technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r30">[30]</xref>. Minor technical adjustments are often required with robotic surgery, including the use of 8-mm ports and the selection of mesh that can be delivered through smaller trocars. Additionally, many surgeons prefer suture fixation over tacks to reduce costs and leverage the robotic platform&#x02019;s enhanced suturing capabilities.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications&#x000a0;from&#x000a0;minimally invasive&#x000a0;inguinal hernia repair&#x000a0;are identified intraoperatively and postoperatively. Complications can&#x000a0;occur during trocar placement,&#x000a0;dissection, and hernia reduction and include injury to vessels, nerves, bladder, and bowel. Conversion to an open procedure&#x000a0;may be necessary as a result of injury or difficulty with dissection due to adhesions, hernia incarceration, or strangulation.</p>
        <p>Postoperatively, symptomatic hematomas&#x000a0;or seromas may develop, and a patient may experience chronic pain&#x000a0;or delayed presentation of an injury. The nerve most commonly injured is the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve, followed by the genitofemoral nerve. Chronic pain is&#x000a0;associated with damage to the ilioinguinal nerve. Sterility may result from mesh irritation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Hernia recurrence may occur at any time following repair, including days to years later.&#x000a0;Placement of a wide mesh covering the entire myopectineal orifice, with sufficient overlap at the pubic tubercle, and avoiding fixation near nerves and vessels can help minimize the risk of recurrence and chronic pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Factors that increase recurrence risk include comorbidities such as diabetes, obesity, and smoking. Operative factors associated with recurrence include mesh folding and displacement, often secondary to a hematoma or seroma, as well as postoperative infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Recurrence is estimated at 2.4% to 3.8%, and chronic pain at 10% to 30%. Studies have demonstrated both equivalent and reduced postoperative complications following robotic inguinal hernia repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;One&#x000a0;study used an artificial intelligence-generated&#x000a0;overlay model to identify anatomic landmarks during inguinal hernia repair&#x000a0;to help avoid mishaps.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a study of incisional hernia rates at least 5 years postoperatively following robotic inguinal hernia repair showed an 18% incidence of trocar-site hernias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Initial indications for laparoscopic hernia repair were restricted to recurrent and bilateral hernias, but the laparoscopic approach&#x000a0;is now&#x000a0;a well-accepted treatment option for initial, unilateral hernias.&#x000a0;Outcomes&#x000a0;from laparoscopic inguinal hernia are similar to those of open inguinal hernia repair in experienced hands.&#x000a0;The benefits of the laparoscopic approach include shorter recovery and&#x000a0;decreased&#x000a0;incidence of&#x000a0;chronic groin pain.</p>
        <p>Outcomes between TAPP and TEP are similar, and the operative approach is left to the surgeon's discretion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Recurrence rates in laparoscopic versus open inguinal hernia repair are similar.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Mesh fixation methods vary, but&#x000a0;guidelines recommend atraumatic&#x000a0;or no fixation in inguinal hernias, other&#x000a0;than&#x000a0;large direct hernias, which have a higher risk of recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Results from a recent meta-analysis comparing&#x000a0;TEP and TAPP approaches across 10 studies found&#x000a0;TEP repair to be associated with slightly lower rates of recurrence, postoperative pain, wound infections, hematomas, seromas, and recovery time. A meta-analysis's results showed lower rates of seroma formation with sac transection than with full&#x000a0;reduction in transabdominal laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;There were no significant differences between approaches for chronic pain or urinary retention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair&#x000a0;is associated with shorter recovery and reduced pain in older adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Several studies have&#x000a0;evaluated the learning curve&#x000a0;for laparoscopic and robotic inguinal hernia repair, using operative time, short- and long-term complications, and recurrence as metrics. The estimated&#x000a0;number of operative cases to attain proficiency is&#x000a0;between 50 and 250. Ideal&#x000a0;training includes coaching, simulation, and supervised surgical training.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Precise mesh sizing and placement can help prevent recurrence. Recent advancements use CT to tailor the mesh to the dimensions of the myopectineal orifice, resulting in reduced recurrence. The mesh is designed to extend beyond midline, above the conjoint tendon, extend to the anterior superior iliac spine, and, below the pectineal ligament, to the psoas muscle. Bilateral hernia patches&#x000a0;are intended to overlap at the midline. Mesh contracture, if not accounted for, can contribute to the risk of recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Inguinal hernias are an ongoing focus of study and innovation. A recent&#x000a0;study involving bilateral inguinal hernia repair compares mesh with an adherent absorbable polyacetic acid to a three-dimensional polypropylene scaffold that is self-adherent and seals the hernia defect.&#x000a0;Tissue&#x000a0;develops within the scaffold matrix, including nerves, vessels, and connective tissue. In 150 laparoscopic inguinal hernia repairs,&#x000a0;the scaffolding mesh was associated with fewer complications, reduced postoperative pain, and faster recovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;Management of the psychogenic origins of pain, including teaching and introducing nonpharmacologic coping mechanisms, successfully mitigates acute and chronic postoperative pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>Robotic inguinal hernia repairs represent an increasing number of all inguinal hernia repairs performed in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a study&#x000a0;comparing the safety and efficacy of extraperitoneal laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair with robotic-assisted extraperitoneal repair showed no significant differences in complication rates. The&#x000a0;robotic procedure was performed more often for recurrence and incarceration, and among those with a higher body mass index. There was a reduction in postoperative opioid use in the robotic cohort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>The role of robotic surgery continues to expand. The largest impediment to the application of robotic technology is the cost associated with the learning curve and operative time. However, increasing surgeon experience correlates with a significant decrease in operative time in experienced hands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Following training, laparoscopic and robotic inguinal hernia repair do not differ significantly in operative time. Modifications in robotic techniques are ongoing, including the use of single-port systems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;A comparison of single versus multiple ports in laparoscopic and robotic repair found that surgeon experience rather than port number impacts recurrence, hematoma, and seroma formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>There remains no standardized training regimen for robotic platforms, including simulation, live tissue training, and mentored cases, but study results demonstrate high rates of competency, including reduced surgical time, achieved within a relatively short period in a formally structured training program.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a 5-year retrospective study of robotic repair report an incisional hernia rate of 25%, predominantly at the midline trocar site. Machine learning is now integrated into robotic platforms, enabling real-time adjustments and continuous improvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22821.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22821.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Effective minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair requires coordinated interprofessional collaboration and a shared understanding of operative strategy, perioperative management, and complication prevention. Surgeons must possess detailed knowledge of preperitoneal anatomy, proficiency with laparoscopic or robotic instrumentation, and the ability to select the most appropriate approach, such as TAPP and the TEP repair, based on patient characteristics and hernia anatomy. Advanced practitioners assist with preoperative assessment, patient selection, and postoperative follow-up, while perioperative nurses play a critical role in patient preparation, positioning, sterile technique, and intraoperative safety monitoring. Clear communication among the surgical team ensures proper equipment availability, correct port placement, appropriate mesh selection, and adherence to safety protocols, all of which help minimize complications such as nerve or vascular injury and mesh malposition.</p>
        <p>Postoperative care also benefits from coordinated team-based management. Nurses monitor recovery, pain control, and early ambulation, while advanced practitioners and surgeons evaluate for complications such as urinary retention, seroma formation, or recurrence. Pharmacists contribute by optimizing perioperative analgesia, antibiotic prophylaxis, and anticoagulation management when indicated. Patient education delivered collaboratively by the healthcare team, including instructions on activity restrictions, wound care, and warning signs of complications, supports recovery and reduces readmissions. Through effective interprofessional communication and coordinated care pathways, healthcare teams can improve surgical outcomes, enhance patient safety, and ensure a patient-centered approach to minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair.</p>
      </sec>
